---
/**
 * The theme control, as one icon instead of a labelled select.
 *
 * ── WHAT IT REPLACES AND WHY ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Starlight ships `ThemeSelect`: a `<select>` reading `Auto ▾` / `Light ▾` / `Dark ▾`, about 100px
 * of header carrying a word most readers set once and never touch. Oleh, 2026-08-24: make it a
 * toggle, no `Dark` / `Light` text, save the room. The room is what buys the identity control beside
 * it, which is the thing Dan actually asked for.
 *
 * ── IT DRIVES STARLIGHT'S OWN MECHANISM, NOT A SECOND ONE ──────────────────────────────────────
 * Same `localStorage` key (`starlight-theme`), same `documentElement.dataset.theme`, same empty
 * string for "follow the system". That is what keeps Starlight's inline pre-paint script in
 * `ThemeProvider.astro` working untouched: it reads the key before the first paint, and a control
 * that wrote its preference anywhere else would leave the page flashing the other theme on load.
 *
 * ── THE LOSS, NAMED: `AUTO` BECOMES A STARTING STATE RATHER THAN A CHOICE ──────────────────────
 * Three states do not fit in two, so a reader can no longer return to "follow the system" once they
 * have pressed this. Auto survives where it matters most — a first visit with nothing stored still
 * follows the OS, and the empty-string convention is preserved so a cleared storage means auto
 * again — but the control cannot get you back. `apps/web` behaves exactly this way already
 * (`lib/theme.ts`, `getInitialTheme`), so this is the product's existing answer rather than a worse
 * one invented here.
 *
 * ── ONE ICON, WHERE `apps/web` USES SUN + SWITCH + MOON ────────────────────────────────────────
 * Same two Lucide glyphs, same two states, different container: the app's lives in a dropdown panel
 * where width is free, this lives in a header row that already sheds items at the breakpoint. The
 * glyph shown is the theme you would GET, not the one you are in, because a button labels its
 * action; the `aria-label` says so in words for anyone who cannot see which one is drawn.
 */
import Icon from './Icon.astro';
---

<button type="button" class="bo-theme" data-theme-toggle aria-label="Switch to dark theme">
  <span class="bo-theme-i" data-when="light"><Icon name="moon" size={16} /></span>
  <span class="bo-theme-i" data-when="dark"><Icon name="sun" size={16} /></span>
</button>

<script>
  import { wireThemeToggle } from '../lib/theme-toggle';
  wireThemeToggle();
</script>

<style>
  .bo-theme {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 32px;
    height: 32px;
    padding: 0;
    border: 1px solid transparent;
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-8);
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .bo-theme:hover {
    border-color: var(--bo-base-300);
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
  }
  .bo-theme:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--bo-primary);
    outline-offset: -1px;
  }

  /* ONLY ONE GLYPH IS EVER IN THE BOX, and which one is decided by the attribute Starlight already
     writes on `<html>`. Doing it in CSS rather than in the script means the correct icon is in the
     first paint: a script that swapped it would show the wrong glyph for one frame on every load,
     which is the same class of flash the theme provider exists to prevent. */
  .bo-theme-i {
    display: none;
    line-height: 0;
  }
  :global(html[data-theme='light']) .bo-theme-i[data-when='light'],
  :global(html:not([data-theme='dark'])) .bo-theme-i[data-when='light'] {
    display: inline-flex;
  }
  :global(html[data-theme='dark']) .bo-theme-i[data-when='light'] {
    display: none;
  }
  :global(html[data-theme='dark']) .bo-theme-i[data-when='dark'] {
    display: inline-flex;
  }
</style>
